/**
 * @page vfs_concepts VFS Concepts
 * @brief ChibiOS/VFS Concepts
 * This page describes how the VFS works: the file systems and the nodes,
 * the construction of the files tree, the paths, the access to directories
 * and files and the available file system drivers.
 * @note    VFS is under development, this page describes the current
 *          implementation.
 *
 * - @ref vfs_overview
 * - @ref vfs_tree
 * - @ref vfs_paths
 * - @ref vfs_nodes
 * - @ref vfs_drivers
 * - @ref vfs_concurrency
 * - @ref vfs_config
 * .
 *
 * @section vfs_overview Overview
 * The VFS presents file systems of different kinds as a single tree of
 * directories and files. It is made of two kinds of objects:
 * - <b>File systems</b>, objects of classes derived from @p vfs_fs_c. A file
 *   system offers the operations on paths: information on a node, opening
 *   directories and files, deleting, renaming and creating directories.
 *   The file system drivers implement them for each kind of storage, the
 *   overlay and root drivers combine other file systems into a tree.
 * - <b>Nodes</b>, objects of classes derived from @p vfs_directory_node_c
 *   or @p vfs_file_node_c, created by a file system when a directory or a
 *   file is opened.
 * .
 * The classes are generated from the XML descriptions in
 * @p os/vfs/codegen. See @ref vfs_architecture for the object model, the
 * objects lifetime and the path contracts.<br>
 * The functions return codes of type @p msg_t: @p CH_RET_SUCCESS or an
 * error encoding a POSIX error number, for example @p CH_RET_ENOENT. Errors
 * are tested with @p CH_RET_IS_ERROR(). The read and write functions
 * return the number of transferred bytes or an error.<br>
 * The application calls vfsInit() after chSysInit(), then initializes the
 * file system objects and builds the tree.
 *
 * @section vfs_tree The Files Tree
 * A tree is built from an overlay or a root object:
 * - An <b>overlay</b>, of class @p vfs_overlay_driver_c, is a file system
 *   that mounts other file systems as directories at its top level. A file
 *   system is mounted with ovldrvRegisterDriver(), giving the directory
 *   name, and unmounted with ovldrvUnregisterDriver(). Up to
 *   @p DRV_CFG_OVERLAY_DRV_MAX file systems can be mounted on each overlay.
 * - An overlay can also have an <b>overlaid</b> file system, specified at
 *   initialization with an optional path prefix. The paths not matching a
 *   mounted name are passed to the overlaid file system, with the prefix
 *   prepended, so the overlaid file system is the base of the tree.
 * - A <b>root</b>, of class @p vfs_root_c, is an overlay with a current
 *   directory, it is the entry point of an application or of a sandbox.
 * .
 * For example, a FatFS volume as base of the tree with a streams driver
 * mounted as @p /dev:
 * @code
 * ffdrvObjectInit(&ffs_driver);
 * stmdrvObjectInit(&dev_driver, &streams[0]);
 * vfsrootObjectInit(&root_driver, (vfs_fs_c *)&ffs_driver, NULL);
 * msg = ovldrvRegisterDriver(&root_driver, (vfs_fs_c *)&dev_driver, "dev");
 * @endcode
 *
 * @if LATEX_PDF
 * @dot
  digraph example {
    size="5, 7";
    rankdir="LR";
    node [shape=rectangle, fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    root [label="Root\n/"];
    fatfs [label="FatFS driver"];
    dev [label="Streams driver"];
    root -> fatfs [label="overlaid"];
    root -> dev [label="mounted as dev"];
  }
 * @enddot
 * @else
 * @dot
  digraph example {
    rankdir="LR";
    node [shape=rectangle, fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];
    root [label="Root\n/"];
    fatfs [label="FatFS driver"];
    dev [label="Streams driver"];
    root -> fatfs [label="overlaid"];
    root -> dev [label="mounted as dev"];
  }
 * @enddot
 * @endif
 *
 * Listing the top level directory returns the mounted names first, then
 * the entries of the overlaid file system. Renaming between different file
 * systems is not possible and returns @p CH_RET_EXDEV.<br>
 * The file systems are not owned by the overlays and roots mounting them:
 * they must remain valid while mounted and while their nodes are open,
 * and the same file system can be mounted in several overlays or roots.
 * Each sandbox has its own root, assigned with @p sbSetRoot(), so it has
 * its own current directory and mounts.
 *
 * @section vfs_paths Paths
 * A root accepts absolute and relative paths. Relative paths are resolved
 * against the current directory of the root, then the path is normalized:
 * - repeated separators are merged;
 * - @c "." elements are removed;
 * - @c ".." elements remove the previous element, at the top level they
 *   are ignored;
 * - a trailing separator is removed.
 * .
 * Paths longer than @p VFS_CFG_PATHLEN_MAX are rejected with
 * @p CH_RET_ENAMETOOLONG. The file systems below the root receive only
 * absolute normalized paths.<br>
 * The current directory is changed with vfsRootChangeCurrentDirectory(),
 * which verifies that the directory can be opened, and read with
 * vfsRootGetCurrentDirectory(). The storage for the current directory is
 * allocated from the core allocator on the first change.<br>
 * When the root driver is enabled the application defines a variable named
 * @p vfs_root pointing to its root. The functions vfsOpen(), vfsOpenFile(),
 * vfsOpenDirectory(), vfsStat(), vfsUnlink(), vfsRename(), vfsMkdir(),
 * vfsRmdir(), vfsChangeCurrentDirectory() and vfsGetCurrentDirectory()
 * operate on that root. Other roots and file systems are used through
 * their methods, for example vfsFSOpenFile().
 *
 * @section vfs_nodes Directories and Files
 * Opening a directory or a file returns a node:
 * - Nodes are reference counted, vfsClose() releases a reference. The node
 *   is destroyed when its last reference is released.
 * - Each file system driver allocates its nodes from static pools shared by
 *   all its objects, sized by its @p DRV_CFG_XXX_NODES_NUM settings. When a
 *   pool is empty the open returns @p CH_RET_ENOMEM.
 * - vfsGetNodeStat() returns the information on a node: the mode, the size
 *   and, when the @p valid flags say so, the preferred I/O block size, the
 *   allocated blocks and the modification time as an UTC timestamp.
 * .
 * The entries of a directory are read with vfsReadDirectoryFirst() and
 * vfsReadDirectoryNext(), which return 1 for each entry and 0 at the end.
 * An entry contains the mode, the size and the name, up to
 * @p VFS_CFG_NAMELEN_MAX characters.<br>
 * Files are opened with the POSIX open flags: access mode, @p O_APPEND,
 * @p O_CREAT, @p O_TRUNC and @p O_EXCL. A file is accessed with
 * vfsReadFile(), vfsWriteFile(), vfsSetFilePosition() and
 * vfsGetFilePosition(). Sizes and positions are of type @p vfs_offset_t, a
 * signed 32-bit integer.<br>
 * vfsControlFile() performs control operations, the files representing
 * terminals accept the @p VFS_CTL_TTY_XXX operations, the other files
 * return @p CH_RET_ENOTTY.<br>
 * The mode contains the node type, @p VFS_MODE_S_IFREG,
 * @p VFS_MODE_S_IFDIR, @p VFS_MODE_S_IFCHR, @p VFS_MODE_S_IFIFO or
 * @p VFS_MODE_S_IFBLK, and the owner permission bits.
 *
 * @section vfs_drivers File System Drivers
 * | Driver   | Initialization          | Description                      |
 * |----------|-------------------------|----------------------------------|
 * | Root     | vfsrootObjectInit()     | Overlay with a current directory, see @ref vfs_tree. It requires the overlay driver. |
 * | Overlay  | ovldrvObjectInit()      | Mounts other file systems, see @ref vfs_tree. |
 * | Streams  | stmdrvObjectInit()      | A directory exposing streams as files, from a table built with the @p DRV_STREAMS_ELEMENT_XXX() macros. |
 * | ROMFS    | romdrvObjectInit()      | Read-only file system from an image in constant data. |
 * | FatFS    | ffdrvObjectInit()       | FAT file systems through the FatFS library. |
 * | LittleFS | lfsdrvObjectInit()      | Flash file systems through the LittleFS library. |
 *
 * - <b>Streams</b>, each element of the table is a FIFO, backed by a
 *   sequential stream, a regular file, backed by a random access stream
 *   which allows seeking, or a terminal, backed by a TTY interface which
 *   accepts the terminal control operations.
 * - <b>ROMFS</b>, the image is generated as C source by the @p mkromfs tool
 *   in @p tools/mkromfs from a directory tree. Files can be stored as
 *   data, generated by application callbacks or compressed, the compressed
 *   files require @p DRV_CFG_ROM_ENABLE_COMPRESSION.
 * - <b>FatFS</b>, the volumes are mounted with ffdrvMount() and unmounted
 *   with ffdrvUnmount(), up to @p DRV_CFG_FATFS_FS_NUM volumes.
 * - <b>LittleFS</b>, each object is bound to a LittleFS configuration and is
 *   mounted, unmounted and formatted with lfsdrvMount(), lfsdrvUnmount() and
 *   lfsdrvFormat().
 * .
 * The ChibiFS driver is a skeleton under development, its operations are
 * not implemented. The template driver is the starting point for new
 * drivers.
 *
 * @section vfs_concurrency Concurrent Use
 * The VFS does not serialize operations. A root, an overlay or a node is
 * used by one thread at a time, threads sharing one must serialize their
 * calls. An overlay mounted in several roots is shared by all of them.
 * Threads that need concurrent access use separate roots and overlays and
 * share only leaf file systems, as the sandboxes do with their private
 * roots, within the limits of the leaf driver: FatFS requires
 * @p FF_FS_REENTRANT, LittleFS its thread-safe build. See
 * @ref vfs_arch_concurrency.
 *
 * @section vfs_config Configuration
 * The file @p vfsconf.h contains the VFS settings:
 * | Option                          | Meaning                               |
 * |---------------------------------|---------------------------------------|
 * | @p VFS_CFG_NAMELEN_MAX          | Maximum length of a name.             |
 * | @p VFS_CFG_PATHLEN_MAX          | Maximum length of a path.             |
 * | @p VFS_CFG_PATHBUFS_NUM         | Number of shared path buffers, used by operations needing an additional path buffer. |
 * | @p VFS_CFG_ENABLE_DRV_XXX       | Enables a driver.                     |
 * | @p DRV_CFG_XXX                  | Settings of a driver, for example the sizes of its node pools. |
 *
 * The file is updated to the current settings by
 * @p tools/updater/update_vfsconf.sh.
 */
